grandesew <tc13@kuerbis.dnsalias.net> wrote:
> On Mar 1, 7:41 pm, Patric Mueller <bh...@bigfoot.com> wrote:
>
> BTW I finally worked up the courage to sneak a peek at your approach
> in NetHack-De. You've inserted a whole layer of abstraction between
> the program code and the text output. Hats off to your ambition! We
> just started translating and added grammar functions as needed.

Starting such a large project without being certain that it could be
finished, that would have been to much risk for my taste. :-)

So I first collected what sentences NetHack generates and programmed
the framework to fit that.

> Considering the, shall we say, stream-of-consciousness style of
> the NetHack code base, which is a very model of how *not* to program
> for localization, I rejected the idea of abstracting the output text
> right
> away. So we've got more correct German output right now, but when
> your German version is done, 

The grammar part should be finished, so where the strings are
completely translated, the output should be correct.

There might be one or two special cases left in the 15% that aren't
yet translated.

If they don't fit into the current grammar framework (e.g. if
extending it would be too difficult) there's also the possibility to
create custom code to handle it (the function
genitivattribut_zu_wort() is such an example [although this wouldn't
have been needed if the input language could have had hierarchical
structure {but I decided against something like XML because of
technical and practical reasons}]).

> you'll be closer to an internationalizable
> program than we'll ever get. Although how well your abstract grammar
> layer will apply to other languages is an interesting linguistic
> question.

Oh, that answer is easy. It won't work. :-)

One of the results of Computer Linguistics trying to do machine
translation is that you can't create an intermediate abstract language
that works equally well resp. at all for all possible languages.

OTOH it might be easier if you start with NetHack-De if you want to do
another internationalized version. But depending on the language it
might be easier just to start from scratch. If the language doesn't
have a lot of inflections then you probably don't need an elaborate
grammer framework.

